1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,260
 So let's have a look at the integration between Bedrock and CloudWatch logs.

2
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:08,380
 So we're going to go under Settings on the bottom left,

3
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:10,800
 and you have Model Invocation Logging.

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,440
 So here we can definitely enable it,

5
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:18,520
 and then this is going to collect all metadata requests and responses

6
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:21,240
 for all model invocations in your accounts.

7
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:24,480
 So you can select the type of data you want to include with logs,

8
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:27,400
 so it could be text, images, and embeddings.

9
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:30,560
 And then the destination could be Amazon as free buckets,

10
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:32,960
 or it could be CloudWatch only, or it could be both.

11
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:34,880
 So I'm just going to use CloudWatch only,

12
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:37,960
 and then you need to specify a log group name.

13
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:45,680
 So I call this one Bedrock Invocation Logging.

14
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:48,960
 And then I will create a user in your role.

15
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:53,520
 So this is the role that Amazon Bedrock will need to send data to CloudWatch logs.

16
00:00:53,520 --> 00:01:01,160
 So I call this one Bedrock Invocation Logging Role.

17
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:02,240
 OK.

18
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:06,480
 And next, we have, as relocation for larger deliveries.

19
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:10,840
 So in case it's over 100 kilobytes, then it can be published to Amazon as free.

20
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:15,600
 But we don't need this right now, so we're going to just save these settings.

21
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:18,880
 So we get an error saying the specified log group doesn't exist.

22
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:22,160
 So we have to create it manually in CloudWatch in this instance.

23
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:25,040
 Maybe this will be fixed by the time you use this.

24
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,360
 But let's go into CloudWatch logs, log groups.

25
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:31,040
 And then you're going to create a new log group.

26
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:35,360
 And the name is going to be this one that I'm going to copy and paste.

27
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:36,600
 So we can set up some settings.

28
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:38,840
 Do you want the log to expire or not?

29
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:41,760
 But we're just going to click on Create and get going.

30
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:44,200
 OK, so now my log group is created.

31
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:45,440
 It is here.

32
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:48,080
 And let's go ahead in Amazon Bedrock.

33
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:54,460
 And we're going to save these settings one more time.

34
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:58,680
 And we now need to say that we won't use an existing service role.

35
00:01:58,680 --> 00:01:59,280
 That is right here.

36
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:01,160
 So let me refer to this.

37
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:05,520
 This is sometimes a bit annoying when you have issues on the console.

38
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:08,520
 But it was may fix this at some point.

39
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:09,040
 So here we go.

40
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:13,240
 Now we have select the existing role that has been created and save the settings.

41
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:15,240
 And we should be good to go.

42
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:18,240
 OK, so the settings have been saved successfully.

43
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:22,920
 And what I can do now, as I can go in chat, I will select a model.

44
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:25,320
 And I will just click on Run.

45
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:29,320
 And then we're going to get-- so we send an input.

46
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:31,320
 And then we get an output.

47
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:31,920
 And we're good to go.

48
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:33,440
 Now let's wait a little bit.

49
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:36,560
 And then go into CloudWatch logs to see if it appears.

50
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:38,760
 So I'm going into CloudWatch logs.

51
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:40,520
 And we refresh this page.

52
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:42,680
 And we have one log stream here.

53
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:45,560
 Give us bedrock model invocations.

54
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:50,560
 And here we have the information that the permissions have set correctly for Amazon bedrock logs.

55
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:54,440
 And then we get some information about a model invocation.

56
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:56,840
 So we get a lot of information around it.

57
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:01,240
 But we know that, for example, the model ID is Amazon Titan Text Express V1.

58
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:04,160
 This is a way for us to identify the models that we're using.

59
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:06,200
 We get information about the region.

60
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:08,760
 And then we get the messages.

61
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:10,280
 So we have a user that's us.

62
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:12,880
 And we sent this input.

63
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:18,520
 And then we have some information around the configuration for this invocation.

64
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:21,680
 And how many tokens was it, 271?

65
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:24,760
 And the output is this message.

66
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:31,480
 So the assistant role means that it's the model itself and the content is this.

67
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:38,000
 And so again, we get the information that the latency was 4,38 millisecond.

68
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
 We get information around the output token.

69
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,560
 The total token and so on.

70
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:45,960
 So this is very helpful because, as you can see, a lot of information is included here.

71
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:49,080
 And we can use this information later on to debug everything.

72
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:52,280
 For example, we could, for example, run an alarm.

73
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:57,560
 And to look at if the latency is always beneath a specific number.

74
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:02,520
 And if one day the latency reaches a high number, then we may want to send an alert saying,

75
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:05,600
 hey, your latency requirements are a little too high now.

76
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:07,680
 And the user experience may be degraded.

77
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:08,640
 So that's the way of doing it.

78
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:14,080
 But hopefully you get the idea of integrating Amazon bedrock with CloudWatch logs.

79
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:19,840
 So the other thing we can do is go into CloudWatch and go into all metrics and

80
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:21,560
 then click on bedrock.

81
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:26,920
 And you may have more metrics than me, but we have here metrics by model ID.

82
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:29,920
 You can look at it or across all model ID.

83
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:33,680
 And for example, we can have a look at the number of invocations or for

84
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:40,040
 example, the invocation latency. And we can see here that the latency is being plotted on

85
00:04:40,040 --> 00:04:44,360
 this graph. So now, of course, if you have a sustained usage of Amazon bedrock,

86
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:47,480
 then you will see a curve here with multiple data points.

87
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:51,600
 But a lot of metrics are being sent by bedrock into CloudWatch metrics.

88
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:56,840
 And you can then build metrics, graphs, dashboards and alarms on top of it as well.

89
00:04:56,840 --> 00:05:00,440
 In case, for example, the invocation latency gets too high.

90
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:02,400
 So let's say for this lecture, I hope you liked it.

91
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:04,340
 and I will see you in the next lecture.

